Campus Design Oversubscription
	> End-hosts at access layer do not constantly send traffic upstream
		=> Core<-Distribution bandwidth can be much lesser than Distribution<-Access bandwidth
			=> Lesser number Core<-Distribution uplinks than Distribution<-Access uplinks
	> Oversubscription ratio
		> Ratio of uplinking bandwidth on Access than to uplinking bandwidth on Distribution
		> (Disribution<-Access bandwidth) / (Core<-Distribution bandwidth)
		> Recommended Oversubscription
			> 20:1 from Access to Distribution
			> 4:1 from Distribution to Core
	> Usually Core/Distribution switches have separate interfaces, with high port count and low bandwidth, for downlinking to access layer
		and separate interfaces, with low port count and high bandwidth, for uplinking to core layer
	> Many times, to increase uplinking bandwidth, multiple uplinks between the same 2 switches are deployed, to multiply the uplinking bandwidth

				Dist1
				 | |
				 | |		2x Uplinking bandwidth available
				 | |
			       Access1
			      /       \
			    PC1       PC2

		> Although STP blocks one of the links for loop prevention => Availavle uplinking bandwidth remains same
		> STP recieves multiple equal cost paths via same neighbour switch
		> Path through port with lower Port Id (Port Priority + Port Number) on uplinking switch will be choosen as Root path for this switch
		> Other link will be blocked, kept on standby for failover

EtherChannel
	> EtherChannel
		Multiple physical interfaces grouped into a single logical interface, on inter-switch links
	> STP sees this etherchannel as single interface => Does not block any link => Full available bandwidth usable
	> Automatic Load-Balancing and Redundancy with Etherchannel
		> Load-Balancing: Traffic will automatically be load-balanced across all links in etherchannel
		> Redundancy: If a link fails, its traffic will failover to remaining links in etherchannel
	> Also called as
		> Port Channel
		> LAG (Link Aggregation)
		> Link Bundle

NIC Teaming
	> Etherchannel groups interfaces on inter-switch links
	> NIC Teaming groups NIC cards on servers into single logical interface, with single IP => No STP blocking + Automatic Load-Balancing & Redundancy
	> Also called as
		> Bonding
		> NIC Balancing
		> Link Aggregation

EtherChannel Load-Balancing
	> Packets from the same flow always go over the same interface within the PortChannel
		Load-Balancing packets, from same flow, round-robin across all links within PortChannel, might cause packets to arrive out-of-order, data corrupted
	> Load-balancing occurs when traffic from multiple flows is to be passed through the PortChannel, each flow will go over a separate single link
		=> Each flow receives a maximum bandwidth of a single link only

Etherchannel Protocols
	> Etherchannel, for all protocols, is created using 'channel-group' cmd
	> Interface-Channel-Mode is specified along with the 'channel-group' cmd
	> Interface-Channel-Mode depends on the etherchannel protocol being used
	> Etherchannel protocol being used (specified thru IntfChannelMode) match on both ends for portchannel to come up
	1) LACP (Link Aggregation Control Protocol)
		> Open standard
		> Automatic Portchannel creation & maintenence negotiation on both sides
		> Interface-Channel-Mode: 'active' or 'passive' (atleast one side must be 'active' for portchannel to be negotiated)
	2) PAGP (Port Aggregation Protocol)
		> Cisco proprietary
		> Automatic Portchannel creation & maintenence negotiation on both sides
		> Interface-Channel-Mode: 'desirable' or 'auto' (atleast one side must be 'desirable' for portchannel to be negotiated)
	3) Static Etherchannel
		> No automatic Portchannel creation & maintenence negotiation
		> Interface-Channel-Mode: 'on' (both sides must strictly be 'on' for port channel to come up)
		> To turn off, use '#no channel-group <channelNumber> mode on'

Etherchannel Creation using Etherchannel Protocols
	1. Select range of interfaces to be grouped into etherchannel
		#interface range <startIntf> - <endIntf>
	2. Create etherchannel using 'channel-group' cmd
		#channel-group <channelNumber> mode <intfChannelMode>
	3. Etherchannel protocol being used must be same on both ends of portchannel for it to be negotiated
	4. Interface parameters must also match on both sides of portchannel for it to come up
		> Speed & Duplex
		> Access or Trunk
		> Access VLAN on access ports
		> Native VLANs and allowed VLANs on trunk ports
	5. Newly created logical interface (Etherchannel) can be accessed as 'port-channel <channelNumber>' interface, for further interface configurations
		#interface port-channel <channelNumber>

Multi-Chassis EtherChannel
		
		CD1-----CD2
		\\	//
		 \\    //
		   Acc3	
	> Etherchannel combined 2 links into single logical link
	> Multi-Chassis Etherchannel combines multiple etherchannels going from an access switch to a redundant core-distribution switch pair, into a single etherchannel
		=> Entire bandwidth avaiable (otherwise stp blocks 1 of 2 etherchannels)+ Automatic Load-Balancing + Automatic Failover
	> Technologies that support Multi-Chassis Etherchannel are stackwise, vss, vpc, available on higher end cisco switches

Layer 3 EtherChannel
	> Etherchannel can also be configured at layer 3 on a layer 3 switch
	> Similar configuration except
		> Ports are made layer 3 ports through '#no switchport'
		> After creating etherchannel, IP address is added on this logical interface through usual interface configuration
		> '#no shutdown' to bring this logical layer 3 port up

Layer 3 Switches
	> Increasing trend in modern networks is to deploy layer 3 switches in instead of layer 2 switches
	> Making inter-switch links as layer 3 links not layer 2 links ()layer 3 etherchannels instead of layer 2 etherchannels)
	> STP will not be required anymore, load-balancing, path determination, and failover will be handled by routing protocols, which faster in convergence & dont block links

